  {
   "url": "https://www.boeing.com/content/dam/boeing/boeingdotcom/commercial/airports/acaps/747-400_Rev_F.pdf",
   "title": "Boeing, 747-400 Airplane Characteristics for Airport Planning (D6-58326-1 Rev F, December 2024), 2.2.1 General Dimensions: Model 747-400; 2.2.1 plan's flap and aileron lines; 3.3.26 F.A.R. Takeoff Runway-Length Requirements, Model 747SP (flaps 10 and 20); 3.4.3 F.A.R. Landing Runway-Length Requirements, Model 747SP (flaps 30)",
   "accessed": "2026-11-25",
   "note": "The nose, cockpit, section, main-deck windows, doors, wing, nacelles, fin and tailplane heights are the 747-400's measured outlines (shapes/B744-passenger.json; the upper deck is the 747-400F's short one, shapes/B744.json), taken apart and changed by tools/trace/b74s/derive.py as the 747SP pages of D6-58326 say. Flaps: the 747-100/-200's spans and chords (2.2.1's plan) with the SP's single slot, settings off 3.3.26's and 3.4.3's charts."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://www.boeing.com/content/dam/boeing/v2/airports/acaps/747_123sp.pdf",
   "title": "Boeing, 747 Airplane Characteristics for Airport Planning, Models 747-100, -200, -300, SP (D6-58326, May 1984, the 2023 reissue on boeing.com): 2.2.2 General Dimensions, Model 747SP (August 1975); 2.3.3 Ground Clearances, Model 747SP; 2.7.1-2.7.2 Main Entry Door Locations; 2.7.6, 2.7.10-2.7.12 Door Clearances; 7.2.1 Landing Gear Footprint, Model 747SP; 7.2.2 Landing Gear Footprint Data, Models 747-100B, -300B, SP",
   "accessed": "2026-11-25",
   "note": "A scan: 2.2.2 is drawn in lines about 0.35 m thick on a 5 ft grid (about 22 px a meter), too coarse to trace. Its figures (the 56.31 m overall length to the fin's top, 53.87 m to the tail cone, 55.85 m to the tailplane's tip, 59.64 m span, 25.22 m tailplane, 6.5 m width, engines 11.94 m and 21.18 m out), 2.3.3's fin tip 10.76 m above the crown, and the door tables' positions (doors at 9.50, 16.41, 31.60 and 41.35 m; 1984 tables 2.7.1 for the 747-100's) set the plugs, the tail's size and the squeezed aft body; the tailplane's plan and the aft body's taper are read off the scan to about 0.15 m (see the derive script). The JT9D-7 nacelles aren't told apart from the 747-400's RB211s at the scan's resolution, so the 747-400's stand in for them. The SP's single-slotted flaps' track fairings aren't drawn on the scan: the 747-400's four stand in. Confidence medium. Gear: 2.2.2's nose gear 7.75 m from the nose; 7.2.1's 18.99 m from it to the wing gear and 3.07 m on to the body gear, the wing gear's 11.00 m and the body gear's 3.84 m track, tires 1.10 m apart and bogies of 1.37 m (2.2.2's 20.52 m to the gear's middle agrees); 7.2.2's 49 x 17 nose and 46 x 16 main tires. The ground is 5.35 m below the centerline: the mean of 2.3.3's A, H, K, L and M (upper deck, aft crown, fin, tailplane tip, wingtip) less each part's height in the shape, spread from 5.03 m (the tailplane tip) to 5.61 m (the fin). The nacelles are left out (RB211 stand-ins)."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boeing_747SP-21,_Pan_American_World_Airways_-_Pan_Am_AN1014020.jpg",
   "title": "Boeing 747SP-21 N532PA, Pan Am, Paris, 17 July 1985 (Wikimedia Commons, GFDL 1.2)",
   "accessed": "2026-11-25",
   "note": "Left side, nearly side-on: the upper deck's ten windows, placed between doors 1 and 2. Only measurements are taken; nothing of the photo is copied."
  },
